    {
      "turn": 2,
      "doctor_question": "Has this difficulty sitting still and feeling restless been present since you were a child, or did it start more recently in adulthood?",
      "targeted_symptom": "additional_requirement_1",
      "patient_response": "Oh, it’s definitely not a new thing, not at all. I mean, if I really think back on it, it’s been this way for as long as I can remember. Honestly, it goes all the way back to when I was a little girl. I remember—gosh, this must have been second or third grade—my poor mother was always getting these notes sent home from my teachers. And they were always about the exact same things. I was constantly popping out of my seat during lessons. Like, the teacher would be up at the chalkboard explaining fractions or whatever, and I’d just be up, wandering over to the pencil sharpener or looking out the window, just unable to stay glued to that little wooden chair. \n\nAnd it wasn't just the standing up, either. I was always talking over my classmates. If someone else was called on, I’d just blurt out whatever was on my mind because I couldn’t wait my turn. I just had this overwhelming urge to get the words out right that second. And if the teacher was asking a question, I’d be shouting out the answer before she even finished her sentence. I just... I couldn't hold it in. It was like my brain was moving a million miles a minute and my mouth and body were just trying to keep up. So, yes, to answer your question, this whole feeling of being restless and just unable to sit still and wait, it’s been a part of my life since I was a kid. It’s just that now, as a 51-year-old woman in a corporate meeting, it looks a little different than a kid jumping up in the middle of spelling practice, you know? But the internal feeling... that’s exactly the same.",
