    {
      "turn": 3,
      "doctor_question": "Along with that restlessness, do you also struggle with paying close attention to details or staying focused on tasks?",
      "targeted_symptom": "S001",
      "patient_response": "Well, you know, it’s funny you ask that, because for a long time I would have said no, I’m great at my job, I know what I’m doing. But honestly, over the past couple of years... it’s been getting a little embarrassing, to be quite frank. I’ll be looking at these massive vendor spreadsheets—and I mean, they are huge, hundreds of lines of data, pricing tiers, delivery windows, all of that. And I’ll sit there, and I’ll go through it, and I will genuinely think I’ve checked it so carefully. I’ll think, \"Okay, this is perfect, ready to go.\" \n\nBut then, sure enough, our planning coordinator—she’s wonderful, very sharp, bless her heart—she’ll come back to me and point out these little things I just totally glossed over. Like, I’ll have transposed a SKU number, just flipped two digits around without even realizing it. Or I’ll completely miss a freight surcharge buried in the terms, which obviously throws off the entire margin calculation. Or, and this one really gets me, I’ll carry the wrong quantity over into the final purchase order. Like, I meant to order 500 units, but somehow I typed 50, or 5,000, because I just... I don't know, my eyes just sort of slide right past the details. It’s incredibly frustrating because I know the business inside and out, I know how to negotiate, I know what products we need, but when it comes to just sitting there and staring at the tiny numbers and making sure every single detail is exactly right... my brain just kind of skips ahead. I just can't seem to lock in on those little specifics lately without making these silly, careless errors.",
